{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And behold, an olive leaf torn off in her bill.</b> Once destruction and restoration were decreed upon man and upon all that is on the earth, it goes without saying that the trees were uprooted and wiped out completely, so much so that our Rabbis, of blessed memory, had to say that even the cone of the millstones was wiped out in the Flood, and they expounded on this the verse (Job 14:19): ״אבנים שחקו מים״. And there is no need to say that the leaf was withered, and if so, from where did she bring it? They said in the Midrash: Rabbi Levi said — she brought it from the Mount of Anointing, for the land of Israel was not touched by the waters of the Flood, as the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Ezekiel (Ezekiel 22:24): בן אדם אמר לה את ארץ לא מטהרה היא ולא גשמה ביום זעם — 'Son of man, say to her: you are a land that is not cleansed and not rained upon in the day of indignation.' 'Not cleansed' — that is, the springs of the deep were not split open; 'not rained upon' — that no rain from the heavens of the Flood fell upon her. But surely the waters of the Flood that were in the other lands did enter into it, for Scripture testifies here: ויכסו כל ההרים הגבוהים אשר תחת כל השמים. And since the driving rain did not fall from the heavens, which would have broken all the trees, therefore the trees there were not uprooted on account of the waters that came there from the other lands; and therefore she took it from the Mount of Anointing, which is the Mount of Olives. And this is what it says: וידע נח כי קלו המים — 'And Noah knew that the waters had abated,' etc." "<b>And furthermore,</b> they expounded [that] from the Garden of Eden she brought it. And one may wonder at this: from where would Noah know that the waters had abated, given that the waters of the Flood did not enter the Garden of Eden? And the Ramban, of blessed memory, wrote: perhaps its gates were closed so that the waters would not enter it, and when the waters abated they opened — end of his words." "<b>And the word</b> טרף — it should have said טרפה בפיה, but it is an adjective like טרוף, and therefore it has a qamatz. And Scripture should have said והנה עלה טרף בפיה, and this would have been sufficient for Noah's knowledge that the waters had abated, for since she took a leaf from a tree — whichever tree it might be — it is certain that the waters had abated. And why did he need to recognize the name of the tree, that it was an olive? On this our Rabbis, of blessed memory, were roused in their Midrash and said: she hinted to him, saying, 'Better that my sustenance be bitter as an olive in the hand of the Holy One, blessed be He, and not be sweet as honey in the hand of flesh and blood.' They made חכמים בפיה — 'the wise in her bill' — a mode of expression." "<b>And furthermore,</b> they expounded in the Midrash: Rabbi Abbahu said — from the tree of the Garden of Eden she brought it. And she should not have brought anything more precious, whether cinnamon or balsam, but she hinted to say: bitter from under the hand of the Holy One, blessed be He, and not sweet from under your hand. And טרף is from the language of sustenance, in the manner of what is written: הטריפני לחם חקי. And behold, there is an a fortiori inference for all creatures: if for the birds this is preferable, then certainly for human beings, who possess intellect and knowledge, and the trait of shame is spread across their faces, that they do not wish to be sustained one from the other:"], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:11:22.647661482Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}